From: jaewun <jaewun@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] scsi: ufs: don't synchronize_irq in ufshcd_disable_irq during PM

On the AYN Thor's non-MCQ UFS controller a deep (S2RAM) resume occasionally
hard-wedges while bringing the link back up. An always-on PMIC SDAM breadcrumb
that survives the power-cycle pinned the hang to ufshcd_disable_irq(), entered
from ufshcd_hba_stop() on the ufshcd_hba_enable() retry in ufshcd_link_startup().

hba->irq is a threaded, IRQF_ONESHOT interrupt (ufshcd_intr / ufshcd_threaded_intr).
The synchronous disable_irq() runs synchronize_irq(), which waits for the
threaded handler to finish. During the deep-resume PM phase that handler is not
scheduled -- which is exactly why resume-time completions are serviced inline in
hardirq -- and a stale in-flight ufshcd_threaded_intr can also be stuck on the
resuming controller. Either way the resume thread waits forever. This is the
same family as the inline-completion / link-startup-rescue fixes, in a different
shape: synchronize_irq() inside disable_irq() rather than wait_for_completion().

Gate the wrapper: while hba->pm_op_in_progress, mask the IRQ with
disable_irq_nosync() instead of disable_irq(). This covers every PM-reachable
caller on the relink path -- ufshcd_hba_stop() AND the qcom ufs_qcom_host_reset()
PRE_CHANGE hook reached via ufs_qcom_power_up_sequence() -- in one place, so the
wedge cannot simply move from one to the next. is_irq_enabled bookkeeping is
unchanged, so the matching ufshcd_enable_irq() still balances. ufshcd_suspend()
also runs under pm_op_in_progress; it is safe to skip the synchronize there too
because the inline-in-hardirq completion path is active for the whole PM window
(both suspend and resume) so no new threaded handler is woken. Non-PM callers
(clk-gating work) keep the synchronous behaviour.

Signed-off-by: jaewun <jaewun@users.noreply.github.com>
diff --git a/drivers/ufs/core/ufshcd.c b/drivers/ufs/core/ufshcd.c
index af51433..0f35ff5 100644
--- a/drivers/ufs/core/ufshcd.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/core/ufshcd.c
@@ -360,7 +360,24 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(ufshcd_enable_irq);
 void ufshcd_disable_irq(struct ufs_hba *hba)
 {
 	if (hba->is_irq_enabled) {
-		disable_irq(hba->irq);
+		/*
+		 * hba->irq is a threaded, IRQF_ONESHOT interrupt. The synchronous
+		 * disable_irq() runs synchronize_irq(), which waits for the threaded
+		 * handler (ufshcd_threaded_intr) to finish. During a PM operation the
+		 * threaded handler is not relied upon -- resume-time completions are
+		 * serviced inline in hardirq -- and it may be unscheduled (the
+		 * deep-resume PM phase) or stuck on the resuming controller. Waiting
+		 * for it then HARD-WEDGES the resume relink: this is reached from the
+		 * ufshcd_hba_enable() retry in ufshcd_link_startup() via both
+		 * ufshcd_hba_stop() and the qcom ufs_qcom_host_reset() PRE_CHANGE
+		 * hook. Mask without synchronizing while pm_op_in_progress; the
+		 * controller reset and inline hardirq completion servicing handle any
+		 * in-flight state.
+		 */
+		if (hba->pm_op_in_progress)
+			disable_irq_nosync(hba->irq);
+		else
+			disable_irq(hba->irq);
 		hba->is_irq_enabled = false;
 	}
 }
